What each hunk of php-5.0.0-0001-zend-alloc-sublet-hooks.patch does, and why it is there.

The patch is INERT without -DZEND_CAP_SUBLET: the three macros then expand to exactly the
arithmetic they replace, and zend_alloc.c compiles to the same object as the pristine source.
The build asserts that with cmp rather than trusting it, so the physical column's results and
Phase A's hierarchical results are unaffected by the file having been patched at all.

Nothing in PHP's own logic moves. Not REAL_SIZE, not cache_index, not MAX_CACHED_MEMORY, not
the list macros, not the cache policy, not the control flow of any function.

  hunk 1  after the ZEND_DO_* macro chain
          Adds the seam's own block: the include, the three ZEND_DO_* redefinitions, and the
          three ZEND_CAP_* macros with their inert fallbacks. This is the only hunk that adds
          more than one line of code.

  hunk 2  _emalloc, the AG(cache) HIT return
          (void *)((char *)p + sizeof(zend_mem_header) + MEM_HEADER_PADDING)
            -> ZEND_CAP_PAYLOAD(p, size)
          The cache path matters as much as the fresh one: a block handed back from the cache
          must be re-issued under a FRESH revocation node, or the previous tenant's alias is
          still live over the same bytes. That is the one property an allocator reusing bytes
          inside a live outer allocation does not get for free.

  hunk 3  _emalloc, the FRESH return -- same substitution.

  hunk 4  _efree, the header derivation
          (zend_mem_header *)((char *)ptr - 48) -> ZEND_CAP_HEADER(ptr)
          By lookup, not arithmetic. The client's capability is bounded to its own object and
          cannot reach the header at all, which is what makes an under-read below the object
          catchable here when it was not in hierarchical mode.

  hunk 5  _efree, the RETIRE -- the only new statement in the file.
          Placed after CALCULATE_REAL_SIZE_AND_CACHE_INDEX(p->size) and BEFORE the cache
          branch, so one line covers both the park and the real free. Everything below it --
          p->size, p->cached = 1, the park itself, REMOVE_POINTER_FROM_LIST and ZEND_DO_FREE --
          then runs against live memory, exactly as upstream.
          THIS IS WHERE THE PHYSICAL PORT HAD TO DEVIATE. With one capability spanning header
          and payload, revoking the caller's pointer killed the header too, so that port
          hoisted REMOVE_POINTER_FROM_LIST above the revoke and made it unconditional -- a
          change to PHP's own _efree that cost four false CAUGHT verdicts to find and still
          costs CRASH-057 a NO-READING. The split makes it unnecessary.

  hunk 6  _erealloc, the header derivation (p = orig = ...) -> ZEND_CAP_HEADER(ptr).
          `orig` is load-bearing: the failure path does ADD_POINTER_TO_LIST(orig), so the old
          block must still be alive after a failed realloc. zend_cap_block_realloc therefore
          allocates the new block first and retires the old one only on success.

  hunk 7  _erealloc, the payload return -- same substitution as hunks 2 and 3. Note the
          upstream text here has no spaces around the + signs, which is why it is a separate
          hunk rather than part of one replacement.

  hunk 8  _mem_block_check, the header derivation -> ZEND_CAP_HEADER(ptr).
          NOT COMPILED in this configuration: lines 640-786 of the pristine file are inside
          #if ZEND_DEBUG, and ZEND_DEBUG is 0 here. Patched anyway so that "every derivation in
          this file goes through the hook" holds without a caveat, and so a debug build would
          be wrong in a way the geometry assertion catches at compile time rather than subtly
          at run time.

NOT PATCHED, deliberately: line 778's payload derivation inside the same ZEND_DEBUG region
feeds _mem_block_check's argument and is dead for the same reason; and the ZEND_MM /
ZEND_WIN32 branches of the ZEND_DO_* chain, which this tree cannot take (Zend/zend_mm.h:31-36
undefines ZEND_MM unconditionally).
